"Name three ways to take care of your back when you carry a school bag.": "575d9e9233dd",
"Reach behind you and run a finger down the middle of your back. Do you feel a row of bumps? Each bump is the back of one bone of your spine. The spine is the tower that holds you up, from your hips to your head, all day long.": "7854da176e44",
"Reach behind you and run a finger down the middle of your back. Do you feel a row of bumps? Each bump is the back of one bone of your spine. The spine is the tower that holds you up, from your hips to your head, all day long. The tower is not one bone but many. Each one is called a vertebra. There are seven in your neck, twelve in your upper back, and five in your lower back. Below them, five more are joined together into one bone called the sacrum, and at the very bottom is the little tailbone. Altogether a baby starts with about 33, and 24 of them stay separate so that you can bend and twist. Between the separate bones lie 23 soft cushions called discs. A disc has a tough ring on the outside and a soft gel in the middle, a little like a jelly doughnut. The discs let your spine bend, and they soak up the bumps when you run and jump. Do you remember that you are a little taller in the morning than at night? During the day your discs are squeezed, and at night, when you lie down, they fill up again. Look at a spine from the side, and you will see that it is not straight. It curves in at the neck, out at the upper back, and in again at the lower back. A newborn baby has only one long curve. The neck curve forms when the baby learns to hold up its head, and the lower curve forms when the child learns to walk. These curves make the spine stronger and springier, like the bends in a spring. The spine has a second job. Every vertebra has a hole in it, and the holes line up to make a long tunnel. Inside the tunnel runs your spinal cord, the thick bundle of nerves that carries messages between your brain and your body. You met it in the lesson about the brain. The bones of your spine are its armor. So how do you take care of your back? Keep the load light: Taiwan's Ministry of Education suggests that a school bag should weigh no more than one eighth of your body weight. Wear both straps, and pull them snug so that the bag sits high and close. Put the heaviest books next to your back. Lift your phone or book up instead of dropping your head. And move! Your back likes changing position much more than it likes sitting still. Now check your own school bag with the tool below the model.": "917b74ca3804",
